Q: Inline and extern keyword for constants A: <p><code>extern</code> and <code>inline</code> serve different purposes.</p>
<p>Use <strong><code>extern</code></strong> when there should be <strong>one definition</strong> of the variable in a <code>.cpp</code> file:</p>
<pre><code>// header.h
extern const int value;

// source.cpp
const int value = 42;
</code></pre>
<p>Use <strong><code>inline</code></strong> (C++17+) when you want to <strong>define the variable directly in a header</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>// header.h
inline constexpr int value = 42;
</code></pre>
<p>The key difference is that <code>inline</code> allows the same definition to appear in multiple translation units without violating the One Definition Rule, whereas <code>extern</code> requires exactly one out-of-line definition.</p>
